# Raspberry Pi additions, composed after omarchy-base.packages and
# omarchy-aarch64.packages (omarchy-pkg-defaults aarch64-rpi): the Pi firmware's
# boot chain and the hardware every Pi 4-class board (Pi 4, Pi 400, CM4) has.
# install/hardware/rpi/ and vulkan.sh install nothing else, so an image's first
# boot runs its deferred steps offline.

# The Raspberry Pi Foundation's kernel, which the firmware boots straight from
# the FAT /boot partition (kernel8.img, device trees and overlays). Omarchy's own
# aarch64 kernel is built without BCM2711 support. Nothing in the Pi's default
# set builds kernel modules, so its headers (some 20,000 files on the SD card)
# are left for whoever needs DKMS to install.
linux-rpi
# The GPU firmware that boots the board (start4.elf, fixup4.dat), and the
# Wi-Fi and Bluetooth firmware for its Cypress radio. Both come from [alarm].
raspberrypi-bootloader
firmware-raspberrypi
# The Pi's boot package (pi/pkgbuilds/omarchy-rpi-boot, built on the Pi into
# [pimarchy]): hardware setup's setup-boot and the update's update-verify need
# it (docs/lifecycle-dispatch.md), and it grows a flashed image's root.
omarchy-rpi-boot
# vcgencmd, pinctrl and the EEPROM tools' dependencies.
raspberrypi-utils
# The bootloader EEPROM updater omarchy-update-firmware runs on a Pi 4 or 400.
rpi4-eeprom
# VideoCore VI (v3d) Vulkan. It is a platform device, so lspci never finds it.
vulkan-broadcom
# Screen recording: the Pi's display (vc4) and GPU (v3d) are separate DRM
# devices, as on Apple Silicon, where gpu-screen-recorder finds no display device
# to capture on. Whether it captures on a Pi is untested, so carry wf-recorder,
# which omarchy-capture-screenrecording falls back to when it cannot start.
wf-recorder
